  "findings": [
    {
      "evidence": "template/scripts/machine_pool.py `_open_descriptors` skips every fd for which `os.fstat` raises, and `lease()` then yields `Lease(nested=True, fds=<possibly empty>)`. A caller that reaches a nested run through `subprocess.run` without `pass_fds` (the default `close_fds=True` closes the fds, but the environment still carries `DEV_PLATFORM_MACHINE_POOL_LEASE`) therefore runs under a lease it does not hold. Its own children get no fds. If the parent exits first, for example a detached worker that inherited the environment, heavy work runs outside the pool with no error. This conflicts with the stated 'fail explicitly' policy and the design claim that orphaned children keep their tokens. Consider failing, or at least printing a line, when the inherited lease's fds are not all present.",
      "id": "nested-lease-silently-drops-missing-fds",
      "severity": "advisory",
      "summary": "A nested run keeps reusing the parent lease even when none of the lease's file descriptors survived into the process. It does not fail, and it does not warn."
    }
  ],
